    "content": {
      "value": "Option A - Direct API\n---------------------\n\n### Architecture Overview\n\nSFCC cartridge hooks call Omnio REST API directly via SFCC Service Framework. The reverse flow Omnio pushing back to SFCC, uses a **SCAPI custom API endpoint** (SFCC API Extensions framework) hosted on SFCC itself, eliminating any external middleware for the inbound webhook receiver.\n\n#### SFCC → Omnio (outbound)\n\nhttps://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/whiteboard/267845637\n\n#### Omnio → SFCC (inbound via SCAPI custom endpoint)\n\nhttps://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/whiteboard/268992513\n\n**Omnio webhook**→**SCAPI custom API**→**SFCC API extension**→**CustomerMgr.updateProfile**\n\n#### Batch reconciliation (nightly safety net)\n\n**SFCC scheduled job**→**Delta query (c\\_syncStatus)**→**Omnio bulk API**→**Reconcile failures**\n\n| **Pros** | **Cons** |\n| --- | --- |\n| * SCAPI custom endpoint is properly secured via SLAS * All integration logic in one codebase * Custom API versioned and documented via RAML * Faster to build than MuleSoft (1–2 weeks) * Testable locally against sandbox | * Retry queue is custom-built (**c\\_syncStatus** object) * No visual monitoring - must add external logging * Circular loop prevention is manual * Schema validation is manual in handler script |\n\nnote6ca5f39b\n\nAssumption is any failure to post to webhooks are retried once the webhook is available\n\n**Key risk:** SFCC hooks are synchronous by default. If Omnio API is slow or down, the hook timeout can block registration or profile update on the storefront.\n\nAssumption is any failure to post to webhooks are retried once the webhook is available\n\n**Key risk:** SFCC hooks are synchronous by default. If Omnio API is slow or down, the hook timeout can block registration or profile update on the storefront.\n\nOption B - MuleSoft\n-------------------\n\n### Architecture Overview Recommended for ZIMGreen\n\nMuleSoft Anypoint Platform is the integration hub between SFCC and Omnio. SFCC fires lightweight events via cartridge hooks to MuleSoft. MuleSoft owns all transformation, routing, deduplication, retry, and monitoring. Omnio webhooks arrive at MuleSoft - never directly at SFCC. SFCC Data API is called by MuleSoft for the reverse path.\n\n| **Area** | **Topic** | **Details** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Mechanism | How changes flow | Omneo fires a webhook on any customer data change → MuleSoft receives, validates HMAC signature, transforms payload → calls SFCC Customer API to upsert. This is the only viable approach for real-time bidirectional sync. No decision required — this is fixed. |\n| Outbound (SFCC → Omneo) | PWA-triggered changes | SFCC hooks (order placed, profile updated, OTP registration) → MuleSoft → Omneo REST API. MuleSoft owns the transform and retry. SFCC cartridge fires a generic event; MuleSoft decides the Omneo endpoint and payload shape. |\n| Deduplication key | Cross-system join | **Email address** is the canonical key. MuleSoft object store maintains a cross-reference map of `omneoCustomerId ↔ sfccCustomerNo`. Never use either system's internal ID as the join key across the integration boundary. |\n| Ordering risk | Out-of-order webhooks | MuleSoft must compare `lastModifiedDate` on the inbound Omneo webhook against the SFCC record before applying. A stale webhook arriving after a newer update must be discarded — not applied. Silently dropped stale events must be logged in Anypoint for audit. |\n\n#### SFCC → Omnio (outbound)\n\ncenterhttps://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/whiteboard/268337159false600\n\n**SFCC hook**→**Anypoint MQ**→**DataWeave transform**→**Dedupe check**→**Omnio upsert**\n\n#### Omnio → SFCC (inbound via MuleSoft)\n\nhttps://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/whiteboard/268861443\n\n**Omnio webhook**→**MuleSoft HTTP listener**→**Validate + origin check**→**DataWeave → c\\_ attrs**→**SFCC OCAPI PATCH**\n\n#### Error path (both flows)\n\n**Failure**→**3× retry + backoff**→**Dead-letter queue**→**Anypoint alert + dashboard**\n\n| **Pros** | **Cons** |\n| --- | --- |\n| * Already licensed - no new platform cost * Built-in retry, DLQ, circuit breaker * Anypoint monitoring - visual dashboards * DataWeave - robust contract-based transform * Choice router - clean circular loop prevention * SFCC never directly exposed to Omnio * Easy fan-out - add systems without touching SFCC * Integration team owns flows independently | * Requires MuleSoft / DataWeave expertise * Integration logic outside main SFCC repo * MuleSoft outage affects both flow directions * Slower iteration - Anypoint deployment cycle |\n\nComparison\n----------\n\n| Dimension | Direct API + SCAPI Custom | MuleSoft | Recommended |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Platform cost | No new cost | Already licensed | Draw |\n| Build time | 1–2 weeks | 2–3 weeks | Direct API |\n| Inbound receiver | SCAPI custom endpoint - on SFCC infra | MuleSoft HTTP listener | Direct API |\n| SFCC security boundary | Omnio calls SCAPI directly | Omnio never touches SFCC directly | MuleSoft |\n| Retry + DLQ | Custom c\\_SyncStatus object | Built-in Anypoint MQ + DLQ | MuleSoft |\n| Monitoring | More Logs required | Anypoint dashboards built-in | MuleSoft |\n| Schema validation | Manual in handler script | RAML + DataWeave contracts | MuleSoft |\n| Codebase ownership | All in SFCC cartridge | Split - Anypoint + cartridge | Direct API |\n| Local dev / testing | SFCC sandbox only | Needs Anypoint Platform | Direct API |\n| Fan-out to more systems | New cartridge code per system | New MuleSoft flow only | MuleSoft |\n| Scalability | Good to ~20k events/day | Unlimited via Anypoint MQ | MuleSoft |\n| Team expertise | SFCC only | SFCC + DataWeave | Direct API |\n\n41bb35f9578ef90fDECIDEDProceed with **Option B - MuleSoft** as the selected integration approach. Decision recorded by @Manikandan Ramachandran (Deactivated) on .\n\n* Proceed with **Option B - MuleSoft**